<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Deliberately the only entitlement. No network client, no file access outside the
      container, no camera, no microphone, no private API. Together with the absence of any
      TCC-requesting API in the source, this means the app asks the user for nothing.
      Keep it this way: adding a capability here is the only way to widen the attack surface.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      The only path outside the app's own container, and it exists for one reason: tea.conf and the
      settings are files people are meant to open, edit and back up. In the container they sat at a path
      four directories deep under a name derived from the bundle identifier, which is a poor place to ask
      someone to keep their tea schedule.

      This is a "temporary exception" entitlement: Apple documents the family as temporary and rejects it
      in App Store review. This app is not in the App Store and is signed ad hoc, so it works, and the
      exception is limited to one directory it creates itself. Replacing it would mean giving up editing
      the files, which is a worse trade than the one capability it buys.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.home-relative-path.read-write</key>
    <array>
        <string>/.config/eyeleo-mac/</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>